    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>By late 1918 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-empire">German Empire</a> was economically and militarily exhausted. A rebellion of sailors at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wilhelmshaven">Wilhelmshaven</a> on 29 October became the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kiel-mutiny">Kiel mutiny</a> on 3 November, and workers' and soldiers' councils modelled on the soviets of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-revolution">Russian Revolution</a> spread across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. On 9 November 1918 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philipp-scheidemann">Philipp Scheidemann</a> of the Majority Social Democrats proclaimed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weimar-republic">German Republic</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reichstag">Reichstag</a> building, the abdication of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaiser">Kaiser</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wilhelm-ii">Wilhelm II</a> was announced, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-maximilian-of-baden">Prince Max of Baden</a> handed the chancellorship to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/friedrich-ebert">Friedrich Ebert</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armistice-of-11-november-1918">armistice</a> was signed at Compiègne on 11 November.</p><p>A Council of the People's Deputies of three MSPD and three <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/independent-social-democratic-party-of-germany">USPD</a> members governed until January 1919. Ebert and General Wilhelm Groener made the secret Ebert–Groener pact, by which the army pledged loyalty in return for keeping its command. In January 1919 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spartacist-uprising">Spartacist uprising</a> was crushed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/freikorps">Freikorps</a> units, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rosa-luxemburg">Rosa Luxemburg</a> and Karl Liebknecht were killed after their arrests. On 19 January 1919 women voted for the first time in the National Assembly election, in which the MSPD won 37.9%. To avoid the fighting in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> the Assembly met at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weimar">Weimar</a>, from 6 February to 11 August 1919, and adopted the constitution that gave the republic its informal name.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The war left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>'s civilians in dire conditions. Between 100,000 and 250,000 died of disease or starvation while the Allied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naval-blockade">blockade</a> continued until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-versailles">Treaty of Versailles</a>. Industrial production fell to 57 percent of its 1913 value. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/printing-press">Printing</a> money to pay for passive resistance to the occupation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ruhr">Ruhr</a> drove the hyperinflation of 1923: savings became worthless, while debtors and large concerns such as those of Hugo Stinnes, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inflation">Inflation</a> King, gained. The Rentenmark, introduced on 15 November 1923 at one trillion paper marks, stabilised the currency.</p><p>From 1924 to 1929, the Golden Twenties, American loans under the Dawes Plan supported a recovery and real wages grew faster than the cost of living. The republic extended <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/social-insurance">social insurance</a>, introduced the eight-hour day, a 48-hour workweek, unemployment insurance and paid maternity leave in 1927, and built over 2 million new homes between 1924 and 1931. Germany was predominantly Protestant, at 64.1% in 1925, with Catholics at 32.4% and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> at 0.9%. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> was a centre of cabaret, jazz, film and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bauhaus">Bauhaus</a>. When American loans dried up after October 1929, unemployment rose to around six million in 1932.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-versailles">Treaty of Versailles</a> the new army, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reichswehr">Reichswehr</a>, was limited to 100,000 men and the navy to 15,000, with no air force, submarines, tanks or large warships. The Reichswehr was formally founded on 1 January 1921. Under Colonel General Hans von Seeckt, Chief of Army Command from 1920 to 1926, it was largely removed from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reichstag">Reichstag</a>'s control, and many historians call it a "state within the state". During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kapp-putsch">Kapp Putsch</a> of March 1920 Seeckt refused to deploy it against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/freikorps">Freikorps</a>, saying "Reichswehr do not fire on Reichswehr", but it then suppressed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ruhr">Ruhr</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a> of some 50,000 armed workers, in fighting in which an estimated 1,000 insurgents and 200 soldiers died.</p><p>The army evaded the treaty. The Black Reichswehr, a secret reserve organised as labour battalions, was set up in 1921 and dissolved in 1923 after its members attempted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kostrzyn-nad-odra">Küstrin</a> Putsch. From 1925 the Reichswehr secretly cooperated with the Soviet Red Army, testing aircraft, tanks and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poison-gas">poison gas</a> banned by the treaty and training German pilots. After 1926 Kurt von Schleicher sought broad support for rearmament, and under Hindenburg the army leadership helped decide the composition of governments. In 1935 the Reichswehr was absorbed into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wehrmacht">Wehrmacht</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The constitution of 1919 created a federal semi-presidential republic. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reichstag">Reichstag</a> was elected by universal suffrage of citizens aged 20 and over, women included, using party-list proportional representation, while the appointed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reichsrat">Reichsrat</a> represented the states. The president was popularly elected for seven years, appointed and removed the chancellor, commanded the armed forces and, under Article 48, could suspend civil rights and legislate by decree if public order was threatened. The president is often called an Ersatzkaiser, a substitute emperor.</p><p>Parliament was fragmented by class and milieu, and coalitions were short-lived; from 1924 to 1929 three chancellors led seven cabinets. Political violence marked the early years: the right-wing Organisation <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/consul">Consul</a> murdered Finance Minister Matthias Erzberger in 1921 and Foreign Minister Walther Rathenau, attacked also because he was a Jew, in 1922. The Law for the Protection of the Republic followed, but an imperial-era judiciary passed lenient sentences on right-wing offenders. From March 1930 President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paul-von-hindenburg">Paul von Hindenburg</a> used Article 48 to back chancellors without a parliamentary majority: Heinrich Brüning, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franz-von-papen">Franz von Papen</a> and Kurt von Schleicher. In July 1932 Papen ousted the elected Social Democratic government of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The republic's foreign policy was shaped by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-versailles">Treaty of Versailles</a>, under which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> lost about 13% of its territory and 7 million people, its colonies and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alsace">Alsace</a>–<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lorraine">Lorraine</a>, and accepted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-guilt-clause">War Guilt Clause</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-reparations">Reparations</a> of 132 billion Reichsmarks were set in 1921; from 1920 to 1931 Germany paid 23 billion marks. Foreign Minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gustav-stresemann">Gustav Stresemann</a> sought revision by peaceful means: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/locarno-treaties">Locarno Treaties</a> of 1925 settled the western borders, Germany joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/league-of-nations">League of Nations</a> in 1926, the Treaty of Berlin kept relations with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> open, and in 1928 he helped mediate the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kellogg-briand-pact">Kellogg–Briand Pact</a>. He received the 1926 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nobel-peace-prize">Nobel Peace Prize</a>.</p><p>The 1920s saw a cultural flourishing in literature, cinema, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a>, music and architecture: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bauhaus">Bauhaus</a> Building by Gropius, the Grosses Schauspielhaus and the Einstein Tower. Its failure became a lesson: the 1949 Basic Law of the Federal Republic of Germany answered its weaknesses with the constructive vote of no confidence, a 5% threshold for the Bundestag and basic rights that cannot be suspended. The black-red-gold flag named in its constitution was readopted by both German states after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>. Foreign Policy calls it the best-known example of a democracy that ceded to fascism.</p></div><div class="profile-inv"><p class="profile-inv-k">Songs in history</p><p class="profile-inv-l"><a href="/songs/heil-dir-im-siegerkranz">Heil dir im Siegerkranz<span>1795</span></a><a href="/songs/deutschlandlied">Deutschlandlied<span>1841</span></a><a href="/songs/wir-sind-des-geyers-schwarzer-haufen">Wir sind des Geyers schwarzer Haufen<span>1919</span></a><a href="/songs/horst-wessel-lied">Horst-Wessel-Lied<span>1929</span></a><a href="/songs/solidaritatslied">Solidaritätslied<span>1931</span></a></p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-depression">Great Depression</a> from 1929 ended the recovery. Unemployment reached 4 million in 1930, and in the election of September 1930 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nazi-party">Nazi Party</a> took 18.3% of the vote. Brüning's austerity, which cut unemployment insurance and forced prices and wages down by 20%, is now held to have deepened the crisis and the growth of support for Hitler. In the election of 31 July 1932 the Nazis won 37.3% and became the largest party. Hindenburg refused Hitler the chancellorship in August, but after Schleicher's government failed, Papen persuaded him, and on 30 January 1933 he appointed Hitler chancellor of a coalition in which the Nazis held two of ten cabinet seats.</p><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reichstag">Reichstag</a> fire of 27 February 1933 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reichstag-fire-decree">Reichstag Fire Decree</a> suspended civil liberties. On 23 March the Enabling Act passed with 68%, only the SPD voting against, the KPD having been banned. Within months all other parties and the trade unions were gone, and on Hindenburg's death on 2 August 1934 Hitler took the president's powers. The constitution of 1919 was never formally repealed but became a dead letter. Historians debate the causes of the collapse, grouping them as economic problems, institutional weaknesses and the roles of individuals and parties, among them the DNVP's refusal to accept the republic.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weimar-republic">Weimar Republic</a> was the German democracy founded in the revolution of November 1918. It survived uprisings, putsches, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-versailles">Treaty of Versailles</a> and the hyperinflation of 1923, and enjoyed a brief recovery and cultural flowering, but the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-depression">Great Depression</a> and rule by presidential decree opened the way for Hitler, whose Enabling Act ended it in 1933.</em></p>

